        ":question/problem": "What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{e/4}\\frac{1}{4x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(4x).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x}\\cdot 4=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $e/4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\operatorname{ln}\u20614$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e/4}\\frac{1}{4x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{e/4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(4x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}\u20614}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|u|){|}_{\\operatorname{ln}\u20614}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}\u20611-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\operatorname{ln}\u20614)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\operatorname{ln}\u20614)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\operatorname{ln}\u20614)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
